remote: how winch reaches a host and runs there

winch works on a host through one SSH connection, the session, with a
helper program at the other end.

What a host needs:
    - Debian 13, Ubuntu 24.04, or Ubuntu 26.04, on amd64 or arm64.
    - Login as root, or as a user with passwordless sudo.
    - A POSIX login shell that prints nothing on stdout before winch
      starts a command.
    - /var/lib/winch/ on a file system that allows running programs.

  Nothing else needs to be installed. The machine that runs winch is
  Linux or macOS with the OpenSSH client.

SSH settings:
  winch runs your ssh, so ~/.ssh/config applies: Port, IdentityFile,
  ProxyJump, and every other setting. Its Host blocks match the host
  name of the inventory, also when the host has an address. The address
  and the user of the inventory override the SSH config. --user
  overrides both.

    # ~/.ssh/config
    Host *.acme.com
      Port 2222
      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519

    # inventories/production.yaml
    hosts:
      web1.acme.com:
        address: 192.0.2.10
        user: admin

    # winch logs in to 192.0.2.10 on port 2222 as admin,
    # with the key ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519.

  winch also passes these options. They win over the SSH config:

    ConnectTimeout=15                  a host that does not answer fails
    ServerAliveInterval=15             and does not hang the run
    ServerAliveCountMax=3
    ControlMaster=no ControlPath=none  a master connection from the SSH
                                       config, with its own keepalive,
                                       is not used
    RemoteCommand=none                 the SSH config does not replace
    PermitLocalCommand=no              or add to the command of winch
    ClearAllForwardings=yes
    ForwardAgent=no ForwardX11=no

Host keys and passphrases:
  When stdin and stderr are terminals, ssh can ask for a new host key or
  a key passphrase. Sessions then start one at a time, in inventory
  order, so each question belongs to one host. After the start, hosts
  run in parallel.

  Without a terminal, or with --batch, winch passes BatchMode=yes and
  sessions start in parallel. A host that needs a question is then
  unreachable. Accept the host key and load the key into ssh-agent
  first:

    ssh -o HostName=192.0.2.10 -l admin web1.acme.com
    ssh-add ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519
    winch apply site --batch

    # ssh asks no questions; the apply question stays.

  The access checks and the connections after a reboot always use
  BatchMode=yes. Run winch doc workflow for the access checks.

When a connection fails:
  A host is unreachable when ssh fails before the session starts: the
  name does not resolve, the connection is refused or takes more than 15
  seconds, or the host key or the login fails. winch then exits 4 when
  no step failed. For an unknown host key, the error names the ssh
  command to run once. For a refused login, it names ssh-add.

  Only one winch run works on a host at a time. When another run holds
  the host, the error names that run and the process ID of its helper.
  When that run is gone, end the process.

The session:
  A session is one ssh process with the helper at the other end. All
  work on a host goes through it. It opens at the first work of the host
  and stays open across stages. It closes when the host has no more
  work, and at the apply question, which can wait for a long time. The
  access checks open their own connections.

  winch keeps the helper in /var/lib/winch/bin/ and sends it when it is
  missing. The helper exits when the session ends and leaves no process,
  except a confirm_access timer until it ends.

  The helpers for amd64 and arm64 are inside the winch binary. A winch
  built with go install has none: plan, apply, run, and exec fail before
  they contact a host. Install a release.

How commands run:
  Every command on the host, also one that a module runs, such as
  apt-get or systemctl, runs as root in /, with this environment:

    LC_ALL  C.UTF-8
    PATH    /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    HOME    /root
    umask   022

  The commands of cmd, assert, unless, validate, then, health, and the
  certbot hooks run through sh -e, with stdin from /dev/null. A command
  of several lines stops at its first failed line.

  A cmd with dir runs in that directory. A cmd with user runs as that
  user, with the home of that user as HOME. It starts no login session:
  PAM does not run, so the login umask and limits of the user do not
  apply.

The time limit:
  A command stops after 10 minutes, or after the timeout of a cmd step
  in seconds. The helper sends SIGTERM to the command and its child
  processes, and SIGKILL 10 seconds later to those that still run. The
  step fails, and the error shows the last 10 lines of output. apt-get
  in pkg, repo, and upgrade has no time limit.

  When the connection to winch ends, the helper lets the running command
  finish within its limit, and then exits. A package install is not cut
  off in the middle. winch exec is different: on Ctrl-C, or when its
  session breaks, its command gets SIGINT, and SIGKILL after 10 seconds.

What winch leaves on the host:
  winch keeps its files in /var/lib/winch/:

    bin/                     the helper
    handlers/<project>       pending handlers
    then/<project>           pending then commands
    checks/<project>         pending health gates and asserts
    certbot-hooks/<project>  pending certbot hooks
    downloads/<project>      checksum, owner, and group of each unpack
    firewall.list            the firewall table after its last load
    rollback/<project>/      old files for confirm_access, during a run
    reboot                   the boot ID and the error of a failed
                             reboot, until the next boot
    lock                     the session lock

  To remove winch from a host, delete /var/lib/winch/. Pending handlers,
  then commands, health gates, asserts, and certbot hooks are then lost.
  The next run of winch starts from the state of the host.
